TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "PNR offensive efficiency",
    "Pull-up shot effectiveness",
    "Offensive strategic adjustments",
    "Play-type outcome sequencing",
    "League-wide trends in play execution",
    "Unit-level offensive metrics",
    "Defensive suppression",
    "Counter strategy"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to define 'failure' or 'suppression' for a play type (e.g., PNR)?",
    "How to identify 'pull-up' shots from play-by-play data?",
    "How to quantify 'lift' in offensive output (points per possession, frequency)?",
    "How to establish a causal link or 'counter' relationship between play outcomes?",
    "What are appropriate 'units' for analysis in strategic adjustments?",
    "How to control for confounding variables in play sequencing analysis?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No historical evidence was available from prior research."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence was available to establish any findings or limitations for this question."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The derived play-type possession records are crucial for identifying PNR possessions and their outcomes (points).",
    "Identification of 'PULL_UP' shots will likely require parsing play-by-play 'action type' and 'sub-type' fields, as it's not explicitly listed as a 'play type' in the derived data description.",
    "The definition of 'PNR fail' is critical and will need to be operationalized (e.g., 0 points, turnover, or below a certain PPP threshold).",
    "Defining 'units' will be a key decision, likely players or player combinations, given the 'median lift > 2pp' which implies individual performance rather than team-wide.",
    "Establishing a 'counter' relationship implies sequential analysis of possessions."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise definition of 'PNR fail' (e.g., 0 points, below league average PPP, specific negative outcome) is not specified and needs to be standardized.",
    "Whether 'PULL_UP' is explicitly available as a 'play type' or 'action type' in the derived data or raw play-by-play, or if it needs to be inferred.",
    "The exact definition of 'units' for which 'lift' is measured (e.g., individual players, specific lineups, teams).",
    "The baseline for comparison when calculating 'lift' (e.g., PULL_UP PPP after PNR fail vs. PULL_UP PPP in general, or PULL_UP PPP after PNR fail vs. other shot types after PNR fail)."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was retrieved, so it could not change the decision."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.7,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "The precise and consistent identification of 'PNR fail' and 'PULL_UP' shots from the available data may be challenging. While play-type records exist, specific failure conditions or shot types like 'PULL_UP' might require careful parsing and validation from raw play-by-play. Misclassification could lead to inaccurate 'lift' measurements."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "If 'units' are defined as individual players or specific player combinations, there may not be enough instances of 'PNR fail' followed by 'PULL_UP' shots for all units to reliably measure 'lift' with statistical power, especially for the '>=70% of units' criterion. This could lead to underpowered tests for many units."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Many factors other than PNR failure could influence a player's decision to take a pull-up shot in a subsequent possession (e.g., defensive scheme, shot clock pressure, game state, individual player tendencies, coaching instructions). Establishing a causal 'counter' relationship is difficult without robust controls."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior work addressing this specific research question was found in the historical records."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Operationalizing 'PNR fail' (e.g., turnover, 0 points, low PPP) and 'PULL_UP' (distinguishing from other jump shots) from the given data could introduce measurement error, impacting the accuracy of 'lift' calculations. The derived play-type records are helpful but may not contain the granularity needed for these specific definitions without further processing."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Many individual players or specific lineups ('units') might not have a sufficient number of 'PNR fail followed by PULL_UP' sequences to reliably calculate 'lift' or to meet the 'median lift > 2pp' threshold, leading to underpowered analysis for a significant portion of the league."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "The observed sequence of PNR failure followed by a pull-up shot could be influenced by numerous confounding variables (e.g., defensive adjustments, game context, shot clock situation, player skill, tactical preferences) rather than being a direct 'counter' strategy. This makes causal inference challenging."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "While overall league data is substantial, the sample size for specific 'units' (e.g., individual players) might be too small to detect a 'lift > 0' or 'median lift > 2pp' with adequate statistical power for the '>=70% of units' requirement. This could lead to false negatives for legitimate counters or make it difficult to definitively assess the hypothesis."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "The 'lift' metric is derived from points per possession (PPP) or frequency, which itself is an outcome. The reliability of this derived label depends on the accuracy of the underlying play and shot classifications."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.3,
    "rejected": 0.3,
    "untestable": 0.2,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.1
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The average 'lift' in Points Per Possession (PPP) for Pull-Up shots taken by a 'unit' in possessions immediately following a 'PNR fail', compared to a baseline PPP for Pull-Up shots by that same unit, across the NBA. The estimand specifically tests if 'lift' is > 0 in >=70% of units and if the median 'lift' across all units is > 2 points per possession.",
    "unit": "An 'offensive unit' will be defined as an individual player who initiates a PNR possession, and subsequently takes a Pull-Up shot. Only players with a minimum threshold of both PNR fail events and subsequent Pull-Up shots will be included in the analysis to ensure sufficient sample size per unit.",
    "sample": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 complete regular season and playoffs data. The 2025-26 data will be excluded due to incompleteness. A 'PNR fail' will be defined as a PNR possession resulting in 0 or 1 point, or a turnover. A 'PULL_UP' shot will be identified from play-by-play action types (e.g., 'Jump Shot', 'Pullup Jump Shot') and subtypes, potentially requiring further filtering based on dribble counts or shot distance in the derived play-type records. Only possessions immediately following a PNR fail event will be considered for 'lift' calculation.",
    "power": "A minimum of 50 PNR fail events followed by a Pull-Up shot will be required per 'unit' (player) to be included in the '>=70% of units' analysis. For the median lift calculation, all units with at least 20 such sequences will be included. A two-sided t-test or non-parametric test will be used for individual unit lift significance, with an alpha of 0.05. The overall test will require sufficient power to detect the specified league-wide pattern.",
    "controls": "To mitigate confounding, a baseline PPP for Pull-Up shots will be established for each unit from all other non-PNR-fail-contingent Pull-Up shot possessions. Additionally, control variables such as game state (score differential, quarter/period), shot clock remaining, and defensive scheme (if inferable) will be considered in a regression framework if establishing a causal link beyond mere sequencing is attempted. However, for this specific question, the primary focus is on the observed sequential pattern and magnitude of 'lift'.",
    "falsification": "The hypothesis would be falsified if less than 70% of qualified units show a statistically significant 'lift > 0' in Pull-Up PPP after PNR failure, or if the median 'lift' across all qualified units is <= 2 points per possession. A negative 'lift' would also falsify the 'lift > 0' component.",
    "multiplicity": "Given the '>=70% of units' criterion, a multiple testing correction (e.g., Benjamini-Hochberg for False Discovery Rate) will be applied to the p-values for individual unit 'lift > 0' tests to control for Type I errors across units. The median lift calculation will be a single aggregated metric.",
    "decision_rule": "The research question is supported if (1) at least 70% of units (players meeting minimum sample size criteria) demonstrate a statistically significant 'lift > 0' in Pull-Up PPP after PNR fail compared to their baseline Pull-Up PPP, AND (2) the median 'lift' across all such qualified units is greater than 2 points per possession. Both conditions must be met.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 complete seasons (regular season and playoffs)."
  }
}

